      "text": "1) All the queries for top k are heading to DB. - Good solution: We can\nreduce this by using a cache with certain TTL. We pick the TTL based on\nthe eventual consistency of the view updates. Issues with current impl: For\nthe cache hit everything is fine. For the case of cache miss we have huge\nwait time which is not a great user excperience. - Great Solution: Cache\nwith precomputation, we use a cron job that adds data to the cache for\neach window. Issue: What happens if the job fails.\n\n2) Handling the massive amount of writes: 70B views / day => 700k qps -\nWe are using SQL db here. Hence we do sharding based on the video ids to\nread from the kafka. This sharding does increase the complexity for overall\ncomputation of top k. We update the cron to fetch tok for each shard and\nthen we merge the result. - we can further do batching of the views. In the\nsolution the person has mentioned about using flink. we can look into it or\nother aggregation solutions to take care of this.(1 hour based batching)\n\n3) Optimizing the top k queries: Current flow for finding top K. : say our\nwindow is T. For the given window we find the view count for each video in\nthe given window then we take the aggregated view count and pull the top\nK. - A good solution is to maintain aggregated tables for each window.\nHence it will be easier to fetch data from there.\n\n4) Supporting sliding window: This would require some minor changes. We\napply sliding window DSA algo here. we fetch minute based aggregated view\ncount. now for the hour window we add the current minute data and remove\nthe 60th minute contribution.\n\n5) Approximation based computing for performance improvement: - using CMS",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1) All the queries for top k are heading to DB. - Good solution: We can reduce this by using a cache with certain TTL. We pick the TTL based on the eventual consistency of the view updates. Issues with current impl: For the cache hit everything is fine. For the case of cache miss we have huge wait time which is not a great user excperience. - Great Solution: Cache with precomputation, we use a cron job that adds data to the cache for each window. Issue: What happens if the job fails.\n\n2) Handling the massive amount of writes: 70B views / day => 700k qps - We are using SQL db here. Hence we do sharding based on the video ids to read from the kafka. This sharding does increase the complexity for overall computation of top k. We update the cron to fetch tok for each shard and then we merge the result. - we can further do batching of the views. In the solution the person has mentioned about using flink. we can look into it or other aggregation solutions to take care of this.(1 hour based batching)\n\n3) Optimizing the top k queries: Current flow for finding top K. : say our window is T. For the given window we find the view count for each video in the given window then we take the aggregated view count and pull the top K. - A good solution is to maintain aggregated tables for each window. Hence it will be easier to fetch data from there.\n\n4) Supporting sliding window: This would require some minor changes. We apply sliding window DSA algo here. we fetch minute based aggregated view count. now for the hour window we add the current minute data and remove the 60th minute contribution.\n\n5) Approximation based computing for performance improvement: - using CMS",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
